# Claude Response
You've caught a real category error in my mock, and it's worth naming precisely before the Mac team builds on it: I gave you a contingency table wearing the periodic table's costume. Rows × columns of design-time categorical axes is a cross-tab — a coverage checklist. The actual periodic table is a fundamentally different object: one measured ordering axis (atomic number), folded at empirically-discovered points so that recurring behavior stacks vertically. The columns were never an input — they're the output of choosing fold lengths that make similar elements align. And critically, Mendeleev's coordinates were derived from measured properties, not from design intent — which is exactly the distinction your Rule 1 already enshrines: uniqueness is sim behavioral fingerprint, never coordinate distance. So your instinct is doctrinally correct: the true Atlas must live in measured-behavior space, with derived-KPI axes — and the design lattice rides along as labels, never as axes. The grid I made survives, demoted to what it honestly is: the coverage dashboard. Two instruments, two jobs.
And here's the convergence that means this costs less than it sounds: the true Atlas is Adopt D — the MAP-Elites archive. That field's founding paper is literally titled "Illuminating search spaces by mapping elites," and its standard artifact is exactly what you're describing: a plane of behavior descriptors, binned, each bin holding its best occupant. Your periodic-table instinct and your QD adoption were the same object all along.
Your questions, in order. How to quantify the axes: run factor analysis on the 31 anchors' fingerprint vectors to find the dominant variance directions — then name them as interpretable composites rather than shipping raw principal components. The two candidates almost every ARPG player already carries in their head: Tempo (commitment/cadence — a composite of timing class exposure, uptime shape, kill rhythm) and Footprint (spatial signature — range profile × AOE share × mobility). Then the crucial move, straight from your five-stage philosophy: axes are instruments, so they pass a retrodiction gate too — the anchor set must spread meaningfully across the plane, and known-similar canon must land adjacent (the D2 spin builds near each other, casters separating from melee without being told). Axes that scramble the canon's own similarity structure are wrong axes, however pretty. Are two enough: two for the iconic chart, always — but handle the rest with the periodic table's own tricks: a real third composite (likely Autonomy — how much of the kit's output comes from summons/orbitals/procs acting without input) rendered as slices/blocks (the lanthanide move: separate small-multiple panes per Autonomy band), plus color/glyph channels, plus the interactive projection-picker for everything else. Reject literal 3D — occlusion kills both testing legibility and devlog screenshots. Tight clusters: hex-bin the plane. Hexes are honest about continuous space, carry occupancy as fill intensity, and give you "cells" without pretending the underlying space is categorical. Overlay anchor-Voronoi territories — each canon anchor as a capital, its realm the region nearer to it than any other — which handles density narratively (crowded hexes are deep inside a realm; discoveries happen in the marches between realms) and hands the fiction a gift: the sealed regions are cursed ground on the same map. Stability (the thing UMAP-style embeddings fatally lack): compute the projection once from the anchors and freeze it — future kits project into the fixed frame, new seasons add points but never rearrange the map, and only a Stage-3 axis-genesis event mints a numbered new edition. The periodic table's authority partly comes from never rearranging; yours will too.
