Which leaves the deepest of your questions, and the most honest answer to "explore what the periodic table actually does": its essence is earned structure — Mendeleev didn't choose the fold, he discovered that behavior recurs along the ordering axis, and the fold merely displays the recurrence. So don't cosplay the table's shape; test for its precondition. Add to stage (iii): order all certified kits along candidate 1-D composites and measure autocorrelation of behavioral role along the ordering. If recurrence is real — if role-structure cycles as some quantity climbs — then the folds are earned, the literal periodic table of kits exists, and that's a genuinely publishable finding plus the single best devlog post this project could produce. If not, the hex atlas is the honest final form, and "we tested our own metaphor and here's what the genre's space actually looks like" is also a banger post. Either outcome pays; only pretending would cost.
