# Prompt 2
Well, that chart looks great, um, and I think those are rather arbitrarily selected key performance indicators rather than being derived from the processes that you recommended. So they're just placeholders. Is that correct?

# Claude Response
Correct — and it's the right question to nail down before the Mac team touches it, because the mock is lying at two different depths and only one of them is obvious.
The obvious layer: yes, Tempo and Footprint are placeholders. I didn't run factor analysis on anchor fingerprints — no fingerprint data exists yet to run it on. They're my hypotheses for what the dominant variance directions will turn out to be, drawn from genre intuition (they're roughly the two axes every ARPG player's head already uses). Educated guesses standing where derived quantities belong. The little "VALIDATED: retrodicts anchor similarity" tag on the X-axis is aspirational fiction — that's the label the axis gets to wear only after it passes the gate, and I put it there to show where the receipt goes, not to claim the receipt exists.
The less obvious layer, and the one that matters more for the prototype: every position on that map is invented. Whirlwind sits low-left because I reasoned it there, not because a fingerprint projected it there. The realms, the adjacencies, Gravewaltz-in-the-marches — all narrative placement. Which means the mock is a specification of the display contract (hexes, capitals, realms, edition rules, where each visual element goes and what it means) and contains zero information about what the real map will look like. The honest possibility to hold: when the actual factor analysis runs, the dominant axes might not be Tempo and Footprint at all — the anchors might separate most strongly along something less nameable, like Autonomy or burst-vs-attrition damage delivery — and the real Edition I could look structurally different from this frame. That would be the instrument working, not the mock failing.
So the build-order implication for the team is strict: the mock authorizes the renderer, not the axes. The renderer contract (hex field, status ladder, capitals, frozen-frame editions) can be built now against synthetic data. The axes wait for the pipeline: anchor fingerprints → factor analysis → candidate composites → the retrodiction gate (anchors spread meaningfully; known-similar canon clusters without being told) → only then do names go on arrows and the frame freezes as Edition I. If Tempo and Footprint survive that process, lovely — my guesses were good. If they don't, the mock's arrows get new names and nothing else changes. The one thing the team should not do is treat the placeholder axes as decided and start reasoning about kit placement against them — that's exactly the arbitrary-KPI failure the five-stage process exists to prevent, applied one level up: axes are instruments too, and this pair hasn't earned trust yet.
Worth adding a visible "SYNTHETIC DATA — DISPLAY CONTRACT ONLY" watermark to the mock before it circulates? One str_replace and the file can't be misread.